{
  "reply": "The note is by Lena Corven, written in October 2020 at the archive of Tessel Yard. It concerns 30 painted clay inventory tags recovered together from a collapsed storeroom shelf. Their painted marks were partly obscured by a pale surface deposit. The note records a treatment trial: every tag was photographed, then placed in a closed humidity chamber for 15 minutes; loosened deposit was lifted with a dry soft brush, and each tag was photographed again under the same lamp position. No liquid solvent was applied. Tags were handled separately, and fragments were not joined during the trial. A blank visibility entry means the conservator could not assess the painted line, not that the line was absent. After treatment, painted lines on 26 tags were rated clearer than before, while 4 showed no rated improvement. Two of the improved tags also lost small flakes from already cracked margins; these are recorded as handling losses, with no chemical cause assigned. Clarity was judged by comparison with photographs, not by an automated image score, and no lettering was transcribed or translated. One week later, staff checked 3 improved tags selected because their painted areas remained intact; all 3 retained the recorded visibility rating and no new loose flakes were noted. The other tags were not included in that follow-up. The note explicitly limits interpretation: it is not an interpretation of the marks or a reconstruction of the storeroom economy; deposit loosening is a practical observation, not proof of a particular mineral reaction; no chemical analysis of deposit or pigment was performed; there was no untreated comparison group; alternative chamber durations were not compared; the note does not date the tags, identify makers, or connect individual marks to named merchants; and it does not recommend applying the treatment to every painted object in the archive. It concludes that visibility sometimes improved under this procedure, but handling damage remains possible. Apart from Lena Corven, Tessel Yard, October 2020, the 30 tags, and the collapsed storeroom shelf, the provided note contains no personal names, catalog numbers, individual tag IDs, or further storeroom or catalog designations."
}

